pinctrl: qcom: flag the SM8475 gpio function as GPIO

The 7.2.5 pinmux core only recognizes a pin as usable as GPIO when the
function it is currently muxed with carries PINFUNCTION_FLAG_GPIO
(drivers/pinctrl/pinmux.c:pinmux_generic_function_is_gpio()). This driver
inherits .strict = true and .function_is_gpio from pinctrl-msm.c, so with an
unflagged "gpio" function every pin that a device claims through its pinctrl
state and then requests as a GPIO is refused:

  sm8475-tlmm f100000.pinctrl: pin GPIO_0 already requested by ae94000.dsi.0;
    cannot claim for f100000.pinctrl:551
  panel-nt36532 ae94000.dsi.0: error -EINVAL: Failed to get reset-gpios
  panel-nt36532 ae94000.dsi.0: probe with driver panel-nt36532 failed with error -22

The same pattern hits the four CS35L41 reset lines (gpio1/3/87/92, holders
0-0040/0-0043/1-0040/1-0041) and the gpio-keys hall lines (gpio10/23): the
panel never probes, so there is no display, and the installed system would
inherit exactly the same failure.

The downstream tree this file was backported from does not hit this only
because its pinctrl-msm.c advertises neither .strict nor .function_is_gpio.
The 7.2.5 baseline does advertise both, and its own SoC drivers declare the
gpio function with MSM_GPIO_PIN_FUNCTION()
(drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-sm8450.c:1269), which expands to
PINCTRL_GPIO_PINFUNCTION() in pinctrl-msm.h. Use the same macro here, so
function_is_gpio() reports the muxed function as GPIO and the device's GPIO
request becomes the single owner of the pin. The pinctrl state still sets
the pad configuration (drive strength, bias); only its mux claim disappears,
which is what the GPIO request then performs itself.

Verified on the unit: with the flag set, GPIO_0 keeps a single owner, the
panel probes and its reset-gpios request succeeds.

--- a/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-sm8475.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/qcom/pinctrl-sm8475.c
@@ -1275,7 +1275,7 @@
 };
 
 static const struct pinfunction sm8475_functions[] = {
-	MSM_PIN_FUNCTION(gpio),
+	MSM_GPIO_PIN_FUNCTION(gpio),
 	MSM_PIN_FUNCTION(aon_cam),
 	MSM_PIN_FUNCTION(atest_char),
 	MSM_PIN_FUNCTION(atest_usb),
